    "_id": "k57enwbt3x8m993t1wwnvavqk585p6c6",
    "answer": "Joints are classified into three types:\n1. **Fibrous (Immovable) joints**, bones held by dense connective tissue; no movement (e.g., skull sutures).\n2. **Cartilaginous (Slightly movable) joints**, bones joined by cartilage; limited movement (e.g., vertebrae).\n3. **Synovial (Freely movable) joints**, fluid-filled cavity between bones; maximum movement (e.g., shoulder, knee).",
    "difficulty": null,
    "dueAt": null,
    "isDue": true,
    "lapseCount": null,
    "lastRating": null,
    "lastReviewedAt": null,
    "locked": false,
    "nextReviewLabel": null,
    "prompt": "How are joints classified based on the degree of movement they allow?",
    "reps": null,
    "scheduledDays": null,
    "stability": null,
    "state": "new"
  },
  {
    "subjectSlug": "biology-12",
    "chapterSlug": "07-skeletal-system-of-man",
    "chapterTitle": "Skeletal System Of Man",
    "topicSlug": "7-3-joints",
    "topicTitle": "7.3 Joints",
    "_id": "k57704jaj7y73pcskxzat1sywd85qg14",
    "answer": "Fibrous joints are **immovable joints** where bones are held tightly together by dense fibrous connective tissue with no joint cavity.\n\nExamples:\n1. **Sutures** of the skull (e.g., coronal suture)\n2. **Gomphoses**, attachment of teeth to the jaw (tooth socket)",
    "difficulty": null,
    "dueAt": null,
    "isDue": true,
    "lapseCount": null,
    "lastRating": null,
    "lastReviewedAt": null,
    "locked": false,
    "nextReviewLabel": null,
    "prompt": "What are Fibrous joints? Give two examples.",
    "reps": null,
    "scheduledDays": null,
    "stability": null,
    "state": "new"
  },
  {
    "subjectSlug": "biology-12",
    "chapterSlug": "07-skeletal-system-of-man",
    "chapterTitle": "Skeletal System Of Man",
    "topicSlug": "7-3-joints",
    "topicTitle": "7.3 Joints",
    "_id": "k575t2422vzhvmtqwadsse5wj985qx10",
    "answer": "A synovial joint is a freely movable joint characterized by:\n- A **synovial cavity** filled with synovial fluid (lubricant)\n- **Articular cartilage** covering bone ends\n- A **fibrous joint capsule** enclosing the joint\n- **Ligaments** reinforcing the capsule\n\nExamples: shoulder (ball-and-socket), elbow (hinge), knee (hinge).",
    "difficulty": null,
    "dueAt": null,
    "isDue": true,
    "lapseCount": null,
    "lastRating": null,
    "lastReviewedAt": null,
    "locked": false,
    "nextReviewLabel": null,
    "prompt": "What defines a Synovial Joint and what are its key structural features?",
    "reps": null,
    "scheduledDays": null,
    "stability": null,
    "state": "new"
  },
  {
    "subjectSlug": "biology-12",
    "chapterSlug": "07-skeletal-system-of-man",
    "chapterTitle": "Skeletal System Of Man",
    "topicSlug": "7-3-joints",
    "topicTitle": "7.3 Joints",
    "_id": "k57191c97eatza1jbdezn03ap585ppp0",
    "answer": "Ligaments are tough, slightly elastic bands of dense connective tissue that **connect bone to bone**. In synovial joints they stabilize the joint, reinforce the fibrous capsule, and prevent excessive movement that could cause dislocation.",
    "difficulty": null,
    "dueAt": null,
    "isDue": true,
    "lapseCount": null,
    "lastRating": null,
    "lastReviewedAt": null,
    "locked": false,
    "nextReviewLabel": null,
    "prompt": "What is the function of ligaments in a synovial joint?",
    "reps": null,
    "scheduledDays": null,
    "stability": null,
    "state": "new"
  },
  {
    "subjectSlug": "biology-12",
